  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/2/2f/X-Men_Vol_1_1.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "In a private school \"in New York's Westchester county,\" a wheelchair-bound Professor Xavier (\"Professor X\") summons his X-Men, who are revealed to be four \"students\" named Cyclops, Angel, Iceman and Beast.\n\nThe Professor communicates with them mentally and begins putting them through some training exercises, which allows the reader a chance to see what each one of them is able to do, with about an entire page devoted to each one.\n[HANG] * Beast is extremely agile (page 6 reveals he is the oldest)\n[HANG] * Angel has wings and is highly maneuverable and can hover as well\n[HANG] * Iceman has the power of cold (and page 5 reveals he is 16, \"a couple of years younger than the others\")\n[HANG] * Cyclops has force beams that are controlled through his eye visor\n[HANG] * And all the while, the Professor is shown to have telepathic abilities\n\nProfessor X then calls for them to cease, for at that moment, a new student is arriving, \"a most attractive young lady!\"&nbsp; The top of page 8 has three of them admiring the \"redhead\" and \"living doll\" from the window, while the younger Iceman is still shown to be immature and unimpressed with the new girl.\n\nWhen Jean Grey comes in to ask the Professor why she was invited to join the school, we learn about \"mutants,\" who possess \"ex-tra power.\"&nbsp; The 5 students are then finally introduced and given actual names, except that Scott is referred to as \"Slim Summers.\"&nbsp; Jean then demonstrates her ability to mentally manipulate objects via telekinesis.\n\nThe Professor then gives his own background, speculating that he is perhaps the first mutant, and that a childhood accident caused him to lose the use of his legs.&nbsp; But since his power is mental, he's able to keep in touch with his X-Men despite his physical handicap.&nbsp; It's then that the Professor also reveals that their mission is to protect the Earth from the \"evil mutants.\"\n\nAnd on cue, the scene shifts to Magneto, who is the first to refer to \"Homo Superior\" and clue us in that he doesn't think the human race deserves to have dominion over the planet any longer because \"the day of the <i>mutants</i> is upon us!\"\n\nAnd soon, at a missile launching site called \"Cape Citadel,\" a missile veers off target, and guns and tanks begin moving by themselves, all of which are controlled by Magneto, who leaves a note in the sky, taking credit for the mischief.&nbsp; Then he shows up at the base personally to claim it \"in the name of Homo Superior!\"\n\nWhen the news of the crisis reaches the Professor, he summons the X-Men, telling them, \"This will be your baptism by fire\" and to go there and defeat \"the first of the evil mutants!\"&nbsp; They are driven to the airport in the Professor's Rolls Royce and flown to the Cape in his private jet.\n\nCyclops introduces himself to the commander and orders the X-Men to attack -- clearly he's been given the lead here.&nbsp; They penetrate Magneto's shield due to the unexpected blast on it by Cyclops.&nbsp; At first, Magneto is not aware that the \"five costumed youths\" are mutants like himself, so he fires missiles at them, which gives Kirby an excuse to consume a couple of pages showing the X-Men averting and then destroying the missiles.\n\nMagneto vows that he won't underestimate these young people again, and on page 21, he personally heads out to confront them.&nbsp; Page 22 shows that he's finally determined that their powers must also be mutant in origin and declares that \"Not all those with mutant powers are fit to rule the Earth.&nbsp; <i>You</i> must be destroyed!\"\n\nBut his attempt to kill the youths fails, and he heads off to fight another day.&nbsp; The Cape commander thanks the X-Men, and the Professor rejoices that the hours of training were worth it as they fly back to re-join him.",
  "grade_one_liner": "A well‑built world and genuinely imposing first threat give the debut real weight.\nMagneto's stumble is noticeable, but the issue remains solidly strong.",
  "subtitle": "A School, A Mission, and a Villain Kick-Off at Full Volume",
  "review_text": "Being a brand new book with characters who essentially are given no origin story, the book spends its first seven pages demonstrating what each of the existing team members can do (and I've indicated these in the plot summary above).&nbsp; Furthermore, they are being trained by the mysterious Professor X for... <i>something</i>.&nbsp; This deliberate vagueness helps set the tone that even the <i>students</i> don't fully understand the purpose of their training yet.\n\n[ASSET: left | HomoSapiens | width=11%] It's only when Angel says (at the bottom of page 3) that, \"Mistakes are for homo-sapiens,\" that we as readers are clued in on the fact that there [SPAN: severity=info; text=something different about them] that we've not been told yet.&nbsp; It's the first textual hint that these kids aren't just unusually gifted -- they're something categorically different.\n\nThe arrival of the new student, the \"most attractive <i>young lady</i>\" named Jean Grey affords Stan and Jack a clever narrative pause -- one that lets them finally introduce the concept of \"mutants\" during the initial conversation between the Professor and Jean.&nbsp; This was hinted at in this same month's issues ([LINK:1963:FF_Annual_1|FF Annual 1] and [LINK:1963:JIM_96_thor0|JIM #96]), but we're given the full introduction here.\n\nThe momentary pause in the action also allows introductions to be given to all four of the previously-shown characters (now <i>without</i> their uniforms), and we're given their names (Hank McCoy is the Beast, Bobby Drake is Iceman, \"Slim\" Summers is Cyclops, and Warren Worthington III is the Angel).&nbsp; The Professor then introduces Jean as \"Marvel Girl,\" and she is welcomed into the X-Men.\n\nHer arrival and subsequent \"interview\" (as stated on page 9) also allows the Professor to give details about his own background and the actual purpose of the school, and right away, it's clear that this book will be set apart from the others, as the X-Men have a very unique purpose, which is that of protecting mutants from humans, while at the same time protecting humans from mutants of the more evil variety.\n\n[ASSET: left | XmenMissionStatement | width=18%] The Professor's speculation on page 10 that he is perhaps the very <i>first</i> mutant seems to fly in the face of what is stated about the Sub-Mariner in [LINK:1963:FF_Annual_1|FF Annual 1] (published on the <i>same day</i> as this magazine, not to <i>mention</i> what Mad Merlin says about himself in [LINK:1963:JIM_96_thor0|JIM #96], dating him at least a thousand years before <i>that</i> -- it's as if Xavier's claim reads less like a firm declaration and more like a working theory).\n[BR]\n[BR] The Professor also reveals that he's in the wheelchair due to \"a childhood accident.\"\n[BR]\n[BR] He finally reveals the true mission statement and reason for the existence of the X-Men, that of providing a safe place for mutants to learn how to use their powers and to \"protect mankind\" from <i>evil </i>mutants.\n\n[DIVIDER]\nAs if on cue, readers are finally introduced to the actual villain of the story -- the one shown so blatantly on the cover.&nbsp; We find him in a \"secret laboratory near Cape Citadel,\" and pages 11-15 show his mastery over magnetism and the serious threat he is to the base, where he not only can alter the trajectories of launched missiles, but can actually <i>cause</i> their launch.\n\nHe also can control guns, tanks, manipulate dust particles in the air, and repel metal objects as well, allowing him to essentially walk right in to the Cape and announce his takeover, all in the name of \"Homo Superior.\"\n[ASSET: center | CapeCitidelIsMine | width=67%]\n\nFor a first appearance, this is an <i>unusually</i> high level of destructive capability -- <i>far beyond</i> the typical \"test run\" villain you'd expect to see.&nbsp; <b><i>Whoa!</i></b>\n\nAnd Kirby's wonderful close up on his very menacing eyes absolutely knocks it out of the park.\n[BR] You just <i>know</i> this guy is [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold; text=evil incarnate!]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nAs an aside, one might well wonder:\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=bold; text=What has Magneto been <i>doing</i> all this time?]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=bold; text=Why haven't we heard from him before <i>now</i>?] (meaning, before the events of this book).\n\nIt's a fair question, but not one that warrants any point deduction for the very simple reason that this is a debut issue, and it's only natural for Marvel to want to introduce a menace that is tied in some way to the brand new book.\n\n<i>Should</i> Marvel readers have heard of Magneto before this?\n[BR] If he's really this major a threat, then <b><i>probably</i></b>.\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(But this is one of those times where the magazine creators get a free pass.)]\n\nThe text only states that Magneto is motivated by a desire to show how \"the human race no longer deserves dominion over the planet Earth!&nbsp; The day of the <i>mutants</i> is upon us!\" (quoted from page 11).\n\nAnd <i>naturally</i>, this \"day\" turns out to coincide with X-Men issue 1.\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | XmenIntroduction | width=16%] [ASSET: right | CyclopsFullPower | width=24%] The takeover of the base provides the first true test for the new X-Men, who have to introduce themselves to the staff at the Cape, and it seems clear from the very beginning that this Cyclops character gives the orders to the team while out in the field, all while the Professor mentally monitors their progress from afar.\n[BR]\n[BR] But right off the bat, Cyclops proves to be a force to be reckoned with (yet <i>another</i> fantastic panel by Kirby to open up page 19).&nbsp; Only then does Magneto seem to understand that he's being attacked by others who have \"power as super-human as my <i>own</i>!\"\n\nAfter seeing them avoid his missiles, he resorts to full-on villain mode and then tries to destroy them with a container of rocket fuel:\n[ASSET: center | YouMustBeDestroyed | width=67%]\n\n[SOUNDEFFECT: text=WHAM!]&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=(Doesn't this seem like the wrong sound effect for an explosion?&nbsp; <i>WHAM?</i>)]\n\n[ASSET: left | DayIsSaved | width=18%] But after Cyclops saves them, Magneto is forced to retreat (we <i>are</i> out of pages now, after all), and the Cape commander shakes hands with the group and thanks them, saying that \"the name of <i>X-Men</i> will be the most honored in my command!\"\n[BR]\n[BR] The [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=day is saved.]&nbsp; And the team jets back home...\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nMy compliments go to the construction of the first half of the book that was set up in such a way to allow the various members of the team to be introduced, both <i>personally</i> (to the new student of Jean Grey) and in their X-Men guises (via their training session).\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=This was a clever way for Stan and Jack to showcase what the various members of the team could do and still allow the Professor to <i>explain</i> it to Jean, who is new to the team... and to the readers at the same time.]\n\nThe concept of \"mutants\" (born with \"<b>ex</b>tra power\") is brand new and sidesteps the need to provide any origin story, though the Professor's comment on page 10 that his own mutation was caused because his parents both worked on the \"first A-bomb project\" is pure speculation on his part (so no fault of his for not being aware that mutants like the Sub-Mariner and Mad Merlin existed before him).\n\nOf course, the cover advertised [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold; text=Magneto] as \"Earth's Most Powerful Super-Villain,\" so all readers knew what was coming, and no sooner did the Professor essentially state the X-Men's mission statement aloud, than the narrative immediately shifted over to introduce us all to the <i>final</i> player in this issue - [SPAN: severity=error; text=the evil Magneto himself!]\n\nI discussed above why no reduction in grade is warranted simply because Magneto waited until this moment to appear for the first time, but still, if the issue had any obvious flaw, it was showing <i>how quickly</i> this new Magneto character turned against his own kind -- the very \"Homo Superior\" he was at first claiming that it was time for.\n\nThere was no appeal made to them to join forces -- to stand beside him and rule the Earth as its superiors.&nbsp; His <i>ideology</i> suggests he should welcome fellow mutants, yet his <i>behavior</i> shows no interest in coalition-building.\n\nOn the <i>contrary</i>, in the course of four pages (19-22), Magneto goes from noticing their presence (and not initially being aware that they, too, are mutants like himself), to personally trying to destroy them with rocket fuel, all while spouting that \"Not all those with mutant powers are fit to rule the Earth!&nbsp; <i>You</i> must be destroyed!\"\n\nThis somehow seems contrary to his stated mission on page 11, that the \"day of the <i>mutants</i> is upon us!\"\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Magneto should have tried to recruit them, but the page count didn't allow for that level of nuance.)]\n\nThe reader walks away with the impression that this Magneto character isn't simply interested in looking after the well-being of mutants -- to ensure that they rule the Earth.&nbsp; Rather, he seems more annoyed that any of them could possibly disagree with him wanting to take over... and then summarily decides kill them on the spot!\n\nHow <i>dare </i>they spoil his plans of world conquest -- the <i>brats</i>!\n[BR] This decision on his part seemed to come right out of the Super-Villain Playbook [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(patent pending).]\n[BR] It's abrupt enough that it risks flattening Magneto into a stock antagonist rather than the more nuanced figure his rhetoric implies.\n\n[SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=large; text=\"You don't agree with me?&nbsp; Fine - <b>YOU DIE!</b>\"]&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=tiny; fonttype=italic; text=(Geez!)]\n\nI <i>understand</i> this was a debut issue, and the goal was likely more to feature the new X-Men in action against... <b><i>something</i></b>... but it still felt like a diminishment of the otherwise fascinating character of Magneto, who comes across far more like yet another petty megalomaniac than as someone who seems determined to put humans in their place and establish a new day of \"Homo Superior.\"\n\nBut then again.. the cover <i>does</i> state that he's a super-villain, so how could we expect anything less?\n\n[DIVIDER]\nAll this being said, the book still does a <i>lot</i> of things right, the first half in particular, where the introductions are all made a very logical manner, using both the training session and the new member to allow the narration, artwork and dialogue to all cooperate and provide some light backstory, not to even mention the novelty of a school for the training of young mutants.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text= It's an interesting backdrop that is very well-crafted.]\n\nAlso, the villain is given adequate time to demonstrate that he's a serious force to be reckoned with.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=Also well done.]\n\nSo if the final confrontation was a mite too blink-and-it's-over (especially given how much buildup his earlier scenes provided), well then... <i>okay</i>, it <i>is</i> only a first issue after all, and at least the book kicks things off nicely, even if not perfectly.&nbsp; Clearly this Magneto character will be making a return appearance sometime in the future, so in that regard, the book seems to start off on the right foot.\n\nThe scenery and backdrop both leave readers with the sense that we are indeed going to be getting an all-different point-of-view here, which sets the stage for a distinctly different kind of Marvel adventure.\n\n<b>Final Grade Note:</b>\n[BR] Magneto's abrupt turn is a real wobble, but the issue's overall craft -- especially the sharply constructed first half, the clear mission setup, and the genuinely imposing threat at Cape Citadel -- is strong enough that the stumble doesn't meaningfully weaken the reading experience.\n\nThe strengths outweigh the flaw, making this a confident 8.0 rather than a 7.5.\n[BR] (Its status as a novel, debut issue also helped tip the scale upward.)",
  "side_note": [
    "[HANG0] * Page 10 has the Professor saying that he must leave Jean with the others \"to get to know each other better,\" but this seems to last only four panels before he's once again shown with them and continues his explanation of why the X-Men exist.\n\n[HANG0] * Cape Citadel is a fictional US base.\n\n[HANG0] * On page 17, the team is referred to as \"the X-Men and Marvel Girl\" in the text.",
    "[ASSET: right | StrangeTales84 | width=17%] For <i>really</i> old-timers out there, I got a kick out of Michael Havery's letter published in TOS #62 where he points out that a character named \"Magneto\" appeared in <i>Strange Tales</i> #84, with a cover date of May 1961, and wouldn't you know it, he's <i>right!</i>\n[BR]\n[BR] I've included a screenshot of the cover for your amusement, where you can see right there on the cover, in big bold letters, reads:&nbsp; <b><i>This is...MAGNETO!&nbsp; The strangest menace the world has ever known!!!</i></b>\n[BR]\n[BR] And the editors admit he has them \"dead to rights,\" further pointing out that <i>Boy, you must have been reading our mags for centuries</i>!\n\nNote that this wouldn't be the last time the ST #84 issue was mentioned.&nbsp; As late as 1967, readers were still pointing this out.&nbsp; See Stuart Henderson's letter, published in [LINK:1967:XMEN_36|XMEN #36], for example."
  ],
  "decoder_ring": "In the interest of full disclosure, I must point out that I have never been a big X-Men fan.\n\nI began reading comics when Claremont and Byrne were in the midst of their historic run on the title in the late 1970s and did indeed enjoy those issues, but when that ended, the book's direction gradually went south, and I ultimately tired of it (and it was in fact one of the first titles I <i>stopped</i> reading).\n\nHowever, as a comics collector at the time, you had no choice but to keep buying it because [SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=italic; text=that's just what you had to do], whether you liked the title or <i>not</i>.&nbsp; It is my opinion that this [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold; text=dread of missing out] on the part of collectors was ultimately more responsible for why it became a #1 seller than the actual content, but that's another story.\n\nI confess I have not read many of the group's early adventures, so I look forward to that and will try to keep an open mind, as my mission here is to experience these issues in the order they were released to the public.\n\n[DIVIDER]\nFor the record (in case anyone cares), I have always pronounced Magneto's name with the short E sound, because that's how the word \"magnet\" is pronounced.&nbsp; He is like a human \"magnet\" and there is nothing \"neat-o\" about him.&nbsp; I understand that various TV shows and/or movies have the character's name pronounced differently, [SPAN: severity=error; text=but I can hardly be blamed for their <b>error</b>], so you'll only hear the <i>correct</i> pronunciation here.&nbsp; <i>Heh!</i>\n\nI might also point out, that in response to Ronald Ford's letter published in XMEN #29 (1966), Stan writes \"<i>Personally, we really dig Magnetty...</i>\"&nbsp; Clearly, this demonstrates that Stan also pronounced the name with a short E.&nbsp; Let's not pretend otherwise, or he would have written \"<i>Magneety.</i>\"\n\nAnd, none of this even includes Marvel's own <i>Not Brand Echh</i> references, where Magneto's name is <i>intentionally</i> spelled in such a way as to poke fun at it so that it must be pronounced \"Mag-Neat-O\" in what is an <i>obvious</i> parody of its <i>actual</i> pronunciation.",
  "highlight": "A well‑built debut for the X‑Men, and a very <i>loud</i> entrance from the imposing Magneto."
}